  "provenance": {
    "grade": "reconstructed",
    "basis": "Reconstructed standing depiction of August Billings, helper in the Glessner household (inferred: the 1900 census enumerates him as a helper in the rear building behind the house, born January 1875 in Sweden, single; no source read says he was still there on 1 July 1904), standing in the stable yard at the alley end of the court, on the carriage drive just inside the courtyard gates and about a metre off the stable's south wall, facing east into the court. The gates, the drive and the coach house are the house's own (HABS sheets 2-3; Glessner's The Story of a House, 1923). A helper's work in a private stable (cleaning and harnessing the horses, the stable and the carriages) is read from Mrs. Seely's Cook Book (1902); his working dress, a striped shirt in shirt sleeves with braces and a belt, dark trousers and a light felt hat, is built after Frances Benjamin Johnston's groom of about 1902 (T-3484). Where he stands and when, his face, hair and complexion are reconstructed: no picture of him is known.",
    "sources": [
      "census_1900_chicago_t623_roll245",
      "glessner_story_of_a_house_1923",
      "habs_glessner_house_il_1015_drawings",
      "seely_1902_coachman_livery_plates",
      "loc_johnston_1902_tuskegee_horse_and_groom"
    ],
    "liberty": "L418",
    "seed": "pa1904_glessner_john_j_c1900_august_billings:1904:stable-yard-standing:20261107",
    "replaceable_by": "A photograph of August Billings or of the Glessner stable staff, or a record that he was or was not working at 1800 Prairie in 1904; his place in the stable yard and his standing have no documentary claim."
  }
